There he saw dreadful people, wicked and intent on sinful conduct:
all the twice-born were heretics, and śūdras were wandering ascetics;
the wife hates her husband, and the disciple hates his teacher;
the servant kills his master, and the son is intent on murdering his father;
all the twice-born are practically śūdras, and cows are like goats;
the Vedas are reduced to mere popular songs, and sacred rites are treated as ordinary actions;
ghosts, spirits, goblins and the like are the deities granting fruits there,
and these alone do people, devoted to sin and sharp [in deceit], worship with faith;
all are devoted to sexual intercourse, staking their lives for its sake;
giving false testimony, with minds always deceitful;
one thing is in the mind, another in speech, and another in deed, always in the Kali age;
the learning of all is rationalistic debate, and that is honoured in the royal palace;
the arts and knowledges such as singing bring delight to kings;
the low attain honour, and not the noble, in the Kali age;

all the twice-born learned in the Veda become impoverished in the Kali age;
devotion to Viṣṇu scarcely exists among men;
sacred fields will mostly be full of heretics;
śūdras are expositors of dharma, and ascetics have matted locks in Kali;
all mortals are short-lived, devoid of compassion, and deceitful;
all are expositors of dharma, and all delight in receiving [gifts];
they desire worship for themselves, and are given to baseless slander;
all are given to envy when their master arrives at his own house;
a brother violates his sister, and a father indeed his daughter, in Kali;
all are devoted to śūdra women, and all consort with courtesans;
they do not recognize the virtuous, and regard them as great sinners;
in their stubborn malice they expose a single fault of the good;
they speak of the collection of faults of the sinful as virtues;
the devoid of virtues in Kali grasp only at faults;
a leech possessing its nature drinks blood, not milk;

medicinal herbs are destitute of potency, and the seasons are inverted;
famine reigns in all kingdoms; a maiden does not give birth at the proper time;
men take pleasure in the arts of actors and dancers in Kali;
those devoted to the learning of the Vedas and the Upaniṣads, possessing superior virtues,
fools view as servants; they are completely fallen, O king;
all have abandoned funeral rites, and abandoned the rites prescribed by the Veda;
the names of Viṣṇu are never on their tongues;
immersed in erotic sentiment, they sing only its songs;
no service to Viṣṇu, no discourse on scriptures, no consecration for sacrifice, not the least reflection,
no pilgrimage to sacred fords, no virtues of giving—wonderfully, none existed anywhere among the people in Kali.
Seeing them, Dharmavarṇa was greatly frightened and utterly astonished.
Seeing his lineage going to destruction through sin, he went to another continent.
Wandering through all the continents and worlds everywhere,
the wise man once, moved by curiosity, went to the realm of the ancestors.
